Subject: [PATCH 27/27] property.fs: encode+ returned the right length over the wrong bytes
Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

TODO 13.2(c). `encode+` is `nip +`: keep the first address, add the two
lengths. That is not concatenation, it is ADJACENCY-BY-ASSUMPTION --
right only while fragment 2 begins exactly where fragment 1 ends, which
holds only while nothing moves `here` between the two alloc-tree calls.

AND THE LENGTH IS NOT WHAT IT GETS WRONG. 13.2 recorded this as "lies
about the length" on both arches, and re-injecting the defect disproved
that on the first run: `nip +` returns l1+l2, which is exactly right.
What it returns is the wrong ARRAY -- a1 followed by whatever happens to
sit at a1+l1. Measured on amd64 and x86 alike, with one `allot` forced
between `1 encode-int` and `2 encode-int`:

    encode+ length      8          correct
    first decode-int    1          correct
    second decode-int   30302f63   the allot'd gap, read as an integer

A plausible length over the wrong bytes is the LIED rung, and worse than
13.2(b): nothing throws, the array decodes, and every field before the
gap is right. The old entry's claim had never been measured -- its probe
tested ADJACENCY and inferred the rest, which is the same shape as the
two claims corrected one commit earlier.

THE FIX keeps the classic fast path byte for byte, for the adjacent case
every existing caller produces, and concatenates into a third array when
the fragments are not adjacent. 1275 5.3.5.1 asks for the combination of
two encoded arrays; it does not promise the first address back, and no
caller in this tree relies on getting it.

WATCHED TO BITE, and BOTH BRANCHES RUN. A fix whose slow path is never
taken is indistinguishable from no fix, so the probe reports which branch
it got before asserting the result:

    c-fast-ADJACENT  c-fast-len=8  c-fast-i1=3  c-fast-i2=4
    c-slow-NOT       c-slow-len=8  c-slow-i1=1  c-slow-i2=2

Re-injecting `nip +` fails it by name on the slow path's SECOND decode --
not on its length, which is why the assertion had to be the decode. The
length check stays, demoted to what it actually is: a guard against the
new path mis-sizing its allocation.

diff --git a/forth/device/property.fs b/forth/device/property.fs
--- a/forth/device/property.fs
+++ b/forth/device/property.fs
@@ -333,8 +333,43 @@
   swap
   ;
 
+\ LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). TODO 13.2(c).
+\
+\ Upstream is `nip +`: keep the first address, add the two lengths. That is not
+\ concatenation, it is ADJACENCY-BY-ASSUMPTION -- right only while fragment 2
+\ begins exactly where fragment 1 ends, which holds only while nothing moves
+\ `here` between the two alloc-tree calls.
+\
+\ AND THE LENGTH IS NOT WHAT IT GETS WRONG. TODO 13.2 recorded this as "lies
+\ about the length", and the control disproved that: `nip +` returns l1+l2, which
+\ is exactly right. What it returns is the wrong ARRAY -- a1 followed by whatever
+\ happens to sit at a1+l1. Measured on amd64 with one `allot` forced between
+\ `1 encode-int` and `2 encode-int`, both arches alike:
+\
+\     encode+ length      8          correct
+\     first decode-int    1          correct
+\     second decode-int   30302f63   the allot'd gap, read as an integer
+\
+\ A plausible length over the wrong bytes is the LIED rung, and worse than (b):
+\ nothing throws, the array decodes, and every field before the gap is right.
+\ The old entry's claim had never been measured -- its probe tested ADJACENCY and
+\ inferred the rest.
+\
+\ So: keep the classic fast path, exactly as it was, for the adjacent case that
+\ every existing caller produces; and when the fragments are NOT adjacent,
+\ actually concatenate them into a third array. IEEE 1275 5.3.5.1 asks for the
+\ combination of two encoded arrays; it does not promise the first address back,
+\ and no caller in this tree relies on getting it.
 : encode+ ( prop-addr1 prop-len1 prop-addr2 prop-len2 -- prop-addr3 prop-len3 )
-  nip +
+  3 pick 3 pick + 2 pick = if
+    nip +                        \ fragment 2 already follows fragment 1
+    exit
+  then
+  dup 3 pick + alloc-tree >r     ( a1 l1 a2 l2 )   ( R: a3 )
+  over r@ 4 pick + 2 pick move   \ fragment 2 -> a3 + l1
+  3 pick r@ 4 pick move          \ fragment 1 -> a3
+  nip + nip                      ( l1+l2 )
+  r> swap
   ;
 
 : encode-phys ( phys.lo ... phys.hi -- prop-addr prop-len )
